From: OpenWrt HA build <local>
Subject: [PATCH] owsync: the sender's date kept, the hash checked, the database told what arrived

owsync picks, of two copies of a file, the one with the later date. A file
that arrived got the date of its arrival, by the receiver's clock, and
after a session with files the whole directory was scanned again into the
database. Both lose edits.

- With the receiver's clock a few seconds ahead, or with a second edit on
  the sender within the time the first took to arrive, the receiver's
  copy of the first edit is "newer" than the second edit itself: the next
  session undoes the second edit on both nodes. A file now keeps the date
  it has on the sender (the peer's SyncState carries it; nothing is added
  to the wire), and a deletion is recorded with the date of the peer's
  deletion, so both nodes hold the same record.
- The scan after a session took into the database whatever else had been
  edited on this node while the session ran. The poller compares the
  directory with the database, found nothing new and never offered that
  edit to the peer. The session now records the files it wrote and
  removed, and nothing else.
- A file edited on this node between the session's scan and the arrival
  of the peer's copy was replaced by that copy, although the comparison
  had been made with the file as it was before. A file that is no longer
  what the scan saw is now left alone; the next session compares it.
- Content was written as it came. A file that changed on the sender
  between its scan and its reading (an older sender does not check)
  arrived with other content than was compared, or half-written. The
  receiver now takes only what it asked for, each file once, and only if
  its SHA-256 is the one the peer announced; the sender checks the same
  before it sends.

A session in which a file that was asked for did not arrive, or was not
taken or not sent for one of these reasons, runs to its end and returns
OWSYNC_ERROR_INCOMPLETE: nothing is damaged, and a caller knows that
another session is owed. A file too large to be sent at all is no longer
offered to the peer, which would otherwise ask for it in every session.

A date further in the future than two clocks may differ (60 s) was
written while a clock was wrong and wins every comparison until that day
comes; with dates kept it would travel to the peer and stay on both. In a
session, where the peer's clock confirms this node's, such a file gets
the present as its date, and a peer's date in the future is taken as the
present.

A file and a deletion of the same instant left each node with its own
answer for ever (each wanted the other to be strictly newer). The file
wins on both now.

The wire protocol does not change: a node with this patch syncs with a
node without it. A file sent to a node without the patch still gets the
date of its arrival there.

